<!-- ============================================================
ARCHITECTURE PROBLEM
============================================================ -->

<section
  class="case-section"
  id="architecture-problem"
>

  <div class="case-copy">

    <span class="section-kicker gold">
      Integration Problem
    </span>


    <h2>
      The front-end should not become the integration architecture.
    </h2>


    <p>
      The original onboarding solution interacts with
      several enterprise capabilities:
      customer information,
      eligibility,
      KYC/FICA,
      documents,
      credit,
      fulfilment,
      core systems
      and post-onboarding servicing.
    </p>


    <p>
      Connecting the colleague-facing workflow
      directly to every downstream system
      would increase coupling,
      duplicate integration logic
      and make future change harder to govern.
    </p>

  </div>



  <div class="case-layout">


    <article class="featured-case">

      <span class="case-status">
        Architecture Question
      </span>


      <h3>
        Where should orchestration responsibility sit?
      </h3>


      <p>
        The case-management experience should understand
        the business outcome it requires,
        but should not need to understand
        every downstream implementation detail.
      </p>


      <div class="flow dark-flow">

        <span>
          Banker
        </span>

        <span>
          Case
        </span>

        <span>
          Service Request
        </span>

        <span>
          Integration
        </span>

        <span>
          Enterprise Capability
        </span>

        <span>
          Outcome
        </span>

      </div>

    </article>



    <div class="roadmap">


      <details open>

        <summary>
          Current Risk · Point-to-Point Dependency
        </summary>

        <p>
          If the operational application knows
          every downstream system directly,
          changes in one system can create
          unnecessary impact across the journey.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Current Risk · Repeated Transformation Logic
        </summary>

        <p>
          Different systems may represent
          customer, application,
          decision and status information differently,
          forcing multiple applications
          to solve the same mapping problem.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Current Risk · Hidden Exceptions
        </summary>

        <p>
          Technical failures can become
          generic user-facing errors
          without clear operational ownership,
          recovery behaviour
          or visibility.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Target Principle · Stable Service Boundary
        </summary>

        <p>
          The onboarding workflow expresses
          the business intent
          while an integration layer manages
          the cross-system execution required
          to achieve that intent.
        </p>

      </details>


    </div>

  </div>

</section>



