INTEGRATION FLOW
============================================================ -->

<section class="panel">

  <span class="section-kicker">
    Example Integration Sequence
  </span>


  <h2>
    From business trigger to operational outcome.
  </h2>


  <div class="roadmap">


    <details open>

      <summary>
        01 · Trigger
      </summary>

      <p>
        A case milestone,
        business event
        or API request
        indicates that an enterprise capability
        must be invoked.
      </p>

    </details>



    <details>

      <summary>
        02 · Assemble Context
      </summary>

      <p>
        Customer,
        application,
        product,
        case
        and relevant process information
        are assembled into the integration context.
      </p>

    </details>



    <details>

      <summary>
        03 · Invoke Service
      </summary>

      <p>
        Flowgear invokes
        the relevant enterprise service
        or downstream capability.
      </p>

    </details>



    <details>

      <summary>
        04 · Transform Information
      </summary>

      <p>
        Source and target representations
        are mapped while preserving
        the business meaning
        of the information.
      </p>

    </details>



    <details>

      <summary>
        05 · Evaluate & Route
      </summary>

      <p>
        The response determines
        whether the process continues,
        invokes another capability,
        creates an exception
        or returns to a human decision.
      </p>

    </details>



    <details>

      <summary>
        06 · Return Operational State
      </summary>

      <p>
        The case receives
        a meaningful result,
        status
        and next action
        rather than raw technical complexity.
      </p>

    </details>


  </div>

</section>



<!-- ============================================================
EXCEPTION ARCHITECTURE
============================================================ -->

<section class="panel">

  <span class="section-kicker">
    Exception Architecture
  </span>


  <h2>
    Failure is part of the architecture.
  </h2>


  <p>
    A successful integration design
    should explain what happens
    when the normal path does not complete.
  </p>


  <div class="domain-grid two">


    <article class="domain-card">

      <h3>
        Technical Timeout
      </h3>

      <p>
        Determine retry behaviour,
        operational visibility
        and when the case should move
        to manual intervention.
      </p>

    </article>



    <article class="domain-card">

      <h3>
        Invalid Response
      </h3>

      <p>
        Detect responses
        that do not conform
        to the expected service contract
        before corrupting downstream process state.
      </p>

    </article>



    <article class="domain-card">

      <h3>
        Missing Information
      </h3>

      <p>
        Return the process
        to a clear operational task
        identifying which information
        must be corrected or collected.
      </p>

    </article>



    <article class="domain-card">

      <h3>
        Business Exception
      </h3>

      <p>
        Negative eligibility,
        compliance
        or credit outcomes
        should route according
        to explicit business rules
        and ownership.
      </p>

    </article>


  </div>

</section>



<!-- ============================================================
ARCHITECTURE DECISIONS
============================================================ -->

<section
  class="case-section"
  id="decisions"
>

  <div class="case-copy">

    <span class="section-kicker gold">
      Architecture Decisions
    </span>


    <h2>
      Deliberate choices behind the proposed model.
    </h2>


    <p>
      Architecture is not only a diagram.
      The value sits in the decisions,
      trade-offs
      and boundaries behind it.
    </p>

  </div>



  <div class="case-layout">


    <article class="featured-case">

      <span class="case-status">
        Decision 01
      </span>


      <h3>
        Keep workflow and integration responsibilities separate.
      </h3>


      <p>
        The operational application
        should manage the colleague workflow.
        The iPaaS should manage
        cross-system integration responsibility.
      </p>

    </article>



    <div class="roadmap">


      <details open>

        <summary>
          Flowgear is not the system of record
        </summary>

        <p>
          Business information remains owned
          by the appropriate enterprise system.
          Flowgear coordinates movement,
          transformation
          and execution.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Prefer reusable services to journey-specific integrations
        </summary>

        <p>
          Eligibility,
          compliance,
          document,
          credit
          and fulfilment capabilities
          should be reusable
          by future products
          and channels.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Use a common information contract where it reduces coupling
        </summary>

        <p>
          A stable onboarding representation
          can prevent every source
          and target system
          from needing to understand
          every other system's format.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Treat exceptions as operational outcomes
        </summary>

        <p>
          Technical failures
          should become visible,
          owned
          and recoverable operational states.
        </p>

      </details>



      <details>

        <summary>
          Scale through patterns, not one-off integrations
        </summary>

        <p>
          Once one service chain is proven,
          the same orchestration,
          monitoring
          and exception patterns
          can support broader onboarding capabilities.
        </p>

      </details>


    </div>

  </div>

</section>



